<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.10.1: Analyzing Graphs and Statements in Function Notation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01023</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.10.1: Analyzing Graphs and Statements in Function Notation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ef35b2b7-133f-4c53-9044-247d1b37ed59</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>This graph shows the populations of Baltimore and Cleveland in the 20th century. \(B(t)\) is the population of Baltimore in year t. \(C(t)\) is the population of Cleveland in year \(t\).</para>
      <para>
          <media alt="Two functions. year and population.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_02_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Estimate \(B(1930)\) and explain what it means in this situation.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(B(1930)\) is about 800,000. It’s the population of Baltimore in 1930.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_0efe13e0-f6d2-4d04-9fce-340a0254928c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which statement is true: \(B(2000) \geq C(2000)\) or \(B(2000) \leq C(2000)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Answer: \(B(2000) \geq C(2000)\)</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(B(2000) \geq C(2000)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain the reasoning for your response to question 2.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      On the graph, the point that represents \(B(2000)\) is located at a higher location than the point that represents \(C(2000)\) for each function.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="cef364e6-c465-409f-bab7-149701c9cf07" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which statement is true: \(B(1900) = C(1900)\) or \(B(1900) &gt; C(1900)\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Answer: \(B(1900) &gt; C(1900)\)</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: \(B(1900) &gt; C(1900)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Explain the reasoning for your response to question 4.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.
      The blue function representing Baltimore has a higher <emphasis effect="italics">y</emphasis>ue in 1900 than the red-dashed function representing Cleveland does.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Were the two cities’ populations ever the same? If so, when?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Yes. The populations were the same in roughly 1910 and 1943.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
